libcamera: software_isp: keep highlights with exposure headroom and digital gain

The exposure loop only knew the scene average, so a bright lamp in a dim room
clipped to white. The loop now measures the brightest pixels, all but 0.5%,
and keeps them below 90% of the sensor range by exposing the sensor up to
three stops darker than its own solution, taking the stops off the analogue
gain first. It judges exposure on the histogram as the digital gain will
show it, so a scene already at the exposure limit still gets the headroom.
The image processor gives the stops back as digital gain after colour
correction, with a rational shoulder above half of white that brings the
sensor range to white, following the brightest channel so hues hold; at a
gain of 1 it changes nothing. Frames report the gain as DigitalGain.

On the OnePlus 7 Pro, a salt lamp in a dim room went from a white blob to
visible texture: analogue gain 32 became 8 with a digital gain of 4. Two
stops still left most of the lamp's shade clipped in red and green; the
third is for stills merged from bursts, which average the extra noise away.

---
